{"id": 2341693, "name": "STATE v. TEEZEVANT", "name_abbreviation": "State v. Teezevant", "decision_date": "1884-01-15", "docket_number": "", "first_page": "363", "last_page": "364", "citations": [{"type": "official", "cite": "20 S.C. 363"}], "court": {"name_abbreviation": "S.C.", "id": 8894, "name": "Supreme Court of South Carolina"}, "jurisdiction": {"id": 59, "name_long": "South Carolina", "name": "S.C."}, "cites_to": [{"cite": "67 N. C. 59", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "N.C.", "opinion_index": 0}, {"cite": "21 Am. Rep. 7", "category": "reporters:state_regional", "reporter": "Am. Rep.", "opinion_index": 0}, {"cite": "78 Pa. 191", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Pa.", "opinion_index": 0}, {"cite": "1 Bay 155", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "Bay", "opinion_index": 0}], "analysis": {"cardinality": 276, "char_count": 3729, "ocr_confidence": 0.475, "pagerank": {"raw": 1.9916032717174367e-07, "percentile": 0.7418374584773089}, "sha256": "c700efc8f8f52f3baeb121b234263de508917a9bfc1891659bca405882334e21", "simhash": "1:837497dfb1ce28be", "word_count": 653}, "last_updated": "2024-02-27T18:33:51.777074+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": [], "parties": ["STATE v. TEEZEVANT."], "opinions": [{"text": "The opinion of the court was delivered by\nMe. Chief Justice Simpson.\nThe defendant was tried and convicted of murder at the May Term, 1883, in the Court of Sessions for Orangeburg county. When called for sentence, the question \u201cwhether he had anything to say why judgment, should not be pronounced on him,\u201d was omitted. The appeal assigns error on account of this omission.\nThere is no doubt that in capital eases the practice of asking-this question before sentence has been universally recognized and followed in this State from the earliest period of our judicial history. State v. Washington, 1 Bay 155. And it seems that it has been the practice, not simply because it was formal and seemly, but because of legal requirement founded upon wise consideration, and, therefore, necessary and indispensable. Mr. Archbold (1 Cr. Pr. & Pl. 676) says, that in capital cases, before judgment is pronounced upon the defendant, it is indispensably necessary that he should be asked \u201c if he has anything to say why judgment should not be pronounced on him,\u201d referring to 1 Chitty Crim. L. 700; West v. State, 2 Zabr. 212, and Grady v. State, 11 Geo. 253; and, also, that this should appear upon the record to have been done. 3 Salic. 358; Mill. Comp. 157; 3 Mod. 265.\nThis is necessary, because, on this occasion, the defendant may .allege any ground in arrest of judgment, or he may plead a pardon, &c. 4 JBl. Com. 376. He further says, that the omission to do this in cases of minor felonies is not a sufficient ground for reversing the judgment, provided it appears that the prisoner and his counsel were both in court when the sentence is pronounced, and urge nothing against the judgment or in mitigation of the defendant\u2019s guilt. Grady v. State, 11 Geo. 253. The same doctrine is found in 1 Bish. Cr. Pro., \u00a7 865, where he says that \u201cit is now indispensably necessary, even in clergyable felonies, that the defendant should be asked if he has anything to say why judgment of death should not be pronounced on him.\u201d 4 Burr. 2086; 3 Salk. 398 ; 3 Mod. 265. Under this uniform practice, founded, as we have seen, upon the law, the defendant here had the right to have this question propounded to him before sentence; and, although it was not demanded, yet, in a case of such serious magnitude, the omission to have the question asked, we think, was error.\nNext, as to the effect of this error. We do not see why it should affect the whole proceeding, and, therefore, render a new trial necessary. The error occurred after trial and conviction, and applied to the subsequent proceeding, to wit, the sentence only; and in reason the remedy should extend only so far as the error extended. The weight of authorities sustain this view. 1 Bish. Cr. Pro., \u00a7 1293; McCue v. Commonwealth, 78 Pa. 191; 21 Am. Rep. 7; State v. Johnson, 67 N. C. 59.\nIt is the- judgment of this court that the judgment of the \u25a0Circuit Court, as to pronouncing sentence, be reversed, and that the case be remanded for resentence after the question above is asked the defendant.", "type": "majority", "author": "Me. Chief Justice Simpson."}], "attorneys": ["Mr. Andrew O. Dibble, for appellant.", "Mr. Solicitor Jervey, contra."], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "STATE v. TEEZEVANT.\n1. The failure of the Circuit judge to ask a prisoner convicted of murder,, \u201c If he has anything to say why judgment should not be pronounced on him,\u201d is error, and requires a resentence, although the prisoner did not. demand that this question should be asked.\n2. But as this error affected the sentence only, the prisoner is not entitled to a new trial, but must be resentenced after being so asked.\nBefore Wallace, J., Orangeburg, May, 1883.\nThis was an indictment against William Trezevant for murder. The opinion states the case.\nMr. Andrew O. Dibble, for appellant.\nMr. Solicitor Jervey, contra.\nJanuary 15th, 1884.\nSee State v. Jefcoat (3), post, page 383."}, "file_name": "0363-01", "first_page_order": 401, "last_page_order": 402}